{
  "dom_id": "step-q214216",
  "name": "Question 12",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14268272/assets/2e7fc81a2491260ce507ed1eec1ee23bb2009ea1f983c58e9accef75b658b3ec.png)\n\nWhich of the plots above represents a relation defined on the set $A=\\{\\alpha ,\\beta ,\\delta \\}$ that is both symmetric and antisymmetric?",
  "worked_solution": "First, recall the following definitions:\n\n- A binary relation $R,$ defined on a set $A,$ is *symmetric* if for all $x,y\\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\,⟹\\,(y,x)\\in R.\n$$\n\nIn terms of a plot, if a point $(x,y)$ belongs to the plot, then $(y,x),$ the point symmetric to $(x,y)$ over the diagonal line $y=x,$ should also be on the plot.\n\n- A binary relation $R,$ defined on a set $A,$ is *antisymmetric* if for all $x,y\\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\,\\text{and}\\,(y,x)\\in R\\,⟹\\,x=y.\n$$\n\nSo, only one of the points, either $(x,y)$ or $(y,x),$ can be on the plot of an antisymmetric relation. Note that this condition allows points on the diagonal line $y=x.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's draw the diagonal line $y=x$ and examine our plots.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14268272/assets/ab6813ac1e4f89f7fe02a097cd4f4e302c87de85dbcbe3c295a8d4edd39b3fb0.png)\n\n \n\n- Plot I represents a relation that is both symmetric and antisymmetric. Notice that it has no pairs of the form $(x,y)$ with $x\\ne y.$ As a result, the propositions in both implications above are false. Hence, the implications themselves must be true.\n\n- Plot II represents an antisymmetric relation since the plot contains at most one point from each pair of the points symmetric over the line $y=x.$\n\n- Plot III represents a symmetric relation since the plot is symmetric over the line $y=x.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
